 "title": "Hard vs ablative antifouling: why some hulls stay clean",
 "script": "Why does one boat come out of the water clean, and another covered in barnacles? Often, it's the paint on the bottom.\n\nAntifouling paint stops weed and barnacles by releasing a biocide, a chemical they don't like. There are two main types.\n\nHard paint is like a sponge. The biocide slowly leaches out, but the paint film stays. It's tough, and handles speed and scrubbing. But once the biocide runs out, it stops working, and old layers build up.\n\nAblative paint is like a bar of soap. As water flows past, the surface slowly wears away, exposing fresh biocide. Less build-up, but it wears faster on very fast boats, or if you scrub it hard.\n\nSo: a fast boat that gets scrubbed often? Hard paint. A cruiser at normal speeds? Ablative is usually easier.\n\nWhich paint is on your hull? Tell us in the comments, and follow Creative Tracks Marine.",
